# .dockerignore - Docker build-context denylist for the apex (Level 1)
# JavaScript health-endpoint tier, which serves /health on port 3000.
#
# The sibling Dockerfile is single-stage: it starts from node:24-alpine, copies
# this tier's sources into /app, declares EXPOSE 3000 and a HEALTHCHECK that
# probes /health, and runs `node server.js`. Only what that image genuinely
# needs at run time may travel into the context. Everything listed below is
# either local machine state, a build-time-only definition, or an artifact with
# no purpose inside a running container.
#
# Two properties make a small context worth the effort. Excluding the
# repository history is the single highest-value entry here: .git is measured in
# megabytes against a tracked source tree measured in kilobytes, so it dominates
# the upload the daemon receives before the first instruction even runs. And a
# context that carries no local-only state is a context that produces the same
# image from the same commit on any machine, which is what makes the build
# reproducible rather than merely repeatable.
#
# This is an EXPLICIT DENYLIST by design. Do not rewrite it as a catch-all "*"
# followed by "!" re-inclusions: negation ordering is subtle, and one
# mis-ordered line silently drops a file the image cannot run without.
#
# Pattern semantics, so no line here is misread as a shell glob: "*" does not
# cross a "/", so an unanchored pattern matches at the context root only. That
# is sufficient at this tier because the composition is flat - every source file
# sits at the root, and every excluded subtree below is excluded as a whole
# directory, which takes its entire contents with it. There are deliberately no
# "**" patterns and no negations in this file.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NEVER ADD THESE. The first two break the build loudly. The last two break
# nothing loudly, which is exactly what makes them the dangerous ones:
#
#   server.js           The entry point. This is the image's CMD; without it the
#                       container has nothing to run.
#   health.js           The payload builder, request handler and server factory.
#                       server.js requires it directly.
#   package.json        Supplies the DECLARED name and version - the first two
#                       members of the /health response body. health.js reads it
#                       from disk at module load, anchored to __dirname.
#   config/health.json  Supplies the DECLARED host, port and path. health.js
#                       reads it the same way.
#
#   Why the last two are the dangerous ones: their absence does not raise. The
#   CommonJS loader tolerates a MISSING package.json completely (only a
#   MALFORMED one breaks require), so health.js loads, falls through to its
#   compiled-in literal fallbacks, and still answers 200 with a valid four-key
#   body. The HEALTHCHECK goes green and CI passes while the identity is no
#   longer actually declared by this repository. Nothing fails, so only a direct
#   existence check inside the image, or an assertion on the payload's name and
#   version, can catch it.
#
#   So: never write a broad pattern such as *.json, config, or config/* - any of
#   those would capture one or both of them. Note that the "*.test.js" line
#   below is safe for the same class of reason inverted: it cannot match
#   index.js, health.js or server.js, because none of those ends in ".test.js".
#
#   index.js is also copied. Nothing at run time requires it, but keeping it
#   makes the image's /app match the repository, and it costs a few bytes.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Version-control metadata, its rules file, and its submodule declaration. None
# is needed at run time, and .git additionally carries local remote-tracking
# configuration that must never be baked into an image layer.
.git
.gitignore
.gitmodules

# The child submodule's working tree. Each tier of this composition builds its
# OWN image from its OWN sources: Levels 2 and 3 have their own Dockerfiles and
# serve ports 8000 and 8080. Copying this directory in would bloat a Node image
# with Python and Java sources it can neither run nor serve.
#
# This is a BUILD-CONTEXT exclusion and nothing more. It has no bearing on
# version control, where the submodule remains fully tracked and first-class -
# .dockerignore is read only by the Docker daemon and is invisible to Git.
child_repo_10_LOC

# Build-time-only container definitions. The daemon reads these from the context
# before the build begins; a running container has no use for either.
.dockerignore
Dockerfile

# Continuous-integration definitions. Consumed by the CI service straight from
# the repository, never from inside the image.
.github

# Local environment files. A secrets file must never enter an image layer, and
# nothing here parses one at run time in any case: this tier reads process.env
# directly, because the composition carries zero third-party dependencies and so
# has no dotenv loader. The pattern intentionally also covers the tracked
# .env.example, which is documentation rather than runtime input.
.env*

# Installed dependency tree. This tier has none - every module it loads is a
# Node built-in - so this directory should never exist. The line is defensive:
# it stops a stale host node_modules from being copied in and shadowing that
# zero-dependency guarantee.
node_modules

# Test sources. Currently index.test.js, exercised by `node --test` in CI and
# during development. A runtime image ships no test.
*.test.js

# Documentation, including this tier's normative contract document. Both are
# read from the repository; health.js and server.js reference
# docs/health-endpoint.md only in comments and never open it at run time.
docs
README.md

# Execution residue from a local run of the test suite or of npm. None of it is
# reproducible input, and a coverage report or a debug log has no meaning inside
# an image.
coverage
.npm
npm-debug.log*
*.log

# Operating-system metadata that file managers leave behind. Never content.
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# Note on what is deliberately NOT excluded: .nvmrc stays in the context. It
# pins the Node version for contributors and for the workflow's
# node-version-file input, and the image's own Node version comes from the
# node:24-alpine base tag instead, so it is unused at run time - but it is eight
# bytes, it keeps /app consistent with the repository, and leaving it costs
# nothing. Python and Java residue patterns (__pycache__, *.pyc, *.class) are
# likewise absent on purpose: this tier cannot produce them, and Levels 2 and 3
# each exclude their own in their own .dockerignore.
